duction—Airport and Airway Trust Fund</td><td>84</td></tr><tr><td>TF-1—Airport and Airway Trust Fund</td><td>85</td></tr><tr><td>I\n\n43: type=page_footer\n   March 2020\n\n45: type=section_header\n   Table of Contents\n\n46: type=text\n   Introduction—Highway Trust Fund 95\nTF-6—Highway Trust Fund 96\nTF-6A—Highway Trust Fund; Highway Account, Mass Transit Account 98\nIntroduction—Inland W\n\n47: type=text\n   GLOSSARY 118\n\n48: type=text\n   ORDER FORM FOR TREASURY PUBLICATIONS Inside back cover\n\n49: type=text\n   NOTES: Definitions for words shown in italics can be found in the glossary; Detail may not add to totals due to rounding; n.a. = Not available.\n\n50: type=page_footer\n   March 2020\n\n51: type=page_header\n   VI\n\n52: type=table\n   <table><tr><th colspan=\"1\">Federal Fiscal Operations</th><th>March</th><th>June</th><th>Sept.</th><th>Dec.</th></tr><tr><td>FFO-5.—Internal Revenue Re\n\n53: type=section_header\n   Nonquarterly Tables and Reports\n\n54: type=text\n   For the convenience of the “Treasury Bulletin” user, nonquarterly tables and reports are listed below along with the issues in which they appear.\n\n55: type=page_footer\n   March 2020\n\n56: type=section_header\n   FINANCIAL OPERATIONS\n\n57: type=text\n   Profile of the Economy Federal Fiscal Operations Account of the U.S. Treasury Federal Debt Fiscal Service Operations Ownership of Federal Securities U\n\n58: type=page_number\n   3\n\n59: type=title\n   Profile of the Economy\n\n60: type=title\n   [Source: Office of Macroeconomic Analysis] As of February 17, 2020\n\n61: type=section_header\n   Introduction\n\n62: type=text\n   U.S. economic growth maintained its momentum through the end of 2019 and into early 2020, despite a number of headwinds, bringing the record-long reco\n\n63: type=section_header\n   Economic Growth\n\n64: type=text\n   Real GDP increased by 2.1 percent at an annual rate in the fourth quarter, according the advance estimate, following an identical pace of growth in th\n\n65: type=text\n   Real consumer spending slowed to 1.8 percent in the fourth quarter, after rising 3.2 percent in the third quarter and surging 4.6 percent in the secon\n\n66: type=section_header\n   Growth of Real GDP\n\n67: type=text\n   (Quarterly percent change at annual rate)\n\n69: type=text\n   consumption expenditures in Q4 added 1.2 percentage points to growth, making the second-largest contribution of any component of GDP.\n\n70: type=text\n   Although business fixed investment again constrained GDP growth, it posed only a modest drag in the fourth quarter, subtracting 0.2 percentage point f\n\n71: type=text\n   Notably, the change in private inventories posed the largest drag on economic growth in the fourth quarter, subtracting 1.1 percentage points. Albeit \n\n72: type=page_footer\n   March 2020\n\n73: type=page_number\n   4\n\n74: type=page_header\n   PROFILE OF THE ECONOMY\n\n75: type=text\n   retailers' inability to replace inventories during the labor strike at General Motors from mid-September to late October.\n\n76: type=text\n   After several quarters of retrenchment, residential investment activity improved in the second half of 2019, making small positive contributions to re\n\n77: type=text\n   Total government spending rose 2.7 percent at an annual rate in the fourth quarter, accelerating from the 1.7 percent pace in the third quarter. Feder\n\n78: type=text\n   The U.S. trade deficit narrowed significantly in the fourth quarter of 2019, as export growth accelerated for the second consecutive quarter and impor\n\n79: type=section_header\n   Labor Markets and Wages\n\n80: type=text\n   The unemployment rate has remained at or near a half-century low since September 2019. In December 2019, the unemployment rate stood at 3.5 percent, t\n\n81: type=text\n   2013, and the prime-age LFPR increased to 83.1 percent, its highest level since September 2008.\n\n82: type=text\n   Broader measures of unemployment have also continued to improve in